This work propose Group-TopK, a gradient compression method that fully considers the non-uniform distribution of gradients in neural networks by dividing the gradients into multiple groups and keeping only the top-k largest gradient values within each group. This effectively avoids the information loss caused by global TopK under highly sparse gradients, and Group-TopK is more computation-efficient than global TopK, making it more suitable for edge devices with limited computing resources. On Jetson Orin NX and Jetson Nano clusters, Group-TopK achieves up to 2.51 × acceleration over the baseline while maintaining model accuracy with a loss of no more than 2%, and consistently outperforms commonly used Sample-TopK and Global-TopK methods.